.TH SHAKEY 1 "2026-11-17" "shakey 1.0" "Terminal any key"

.SH NAME
shakey \- terminal any key reader

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shakey
.RB [ \-bcns ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR time-in-seconds ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shakey
(shell anykey) is a small C program that waits for a single keypress
without requiring the Enter key.

.PP
When a key is pressed,
.B shakey
writes that key to stdout and ends the sleep early.
If no key is pressed within the specified time,
.B shakey
exits after the timeout.

.PP
.B shakey
behaves like the standard
.B sleep
command, but allows the wait period to be interrupted by a single
keypress and returns that key on stdout.

.PP
Fractional seconds are supported, for example:

.PP
.nf
shakey -t 1.22
.fi

.PP
.B shakey
is part of the
.B shui-tools
collection.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short usage message.

.TP
.BI \-t " time"
Time to sleep in seconds; fractional values are allowed.

If set to
.BR 0 ,
the maximum wait time of one year is used.

.TP
.BI \-p " text"
Display
.I text
as a prompt.

.TP
.B \-b
Display the prompt in reverse video.

.TP
.B \-c
Show the cursor.

.TP
.B \-n
Output the hexadecimal key sequence.

.TP
.B \-s
Output the key mapping.

.PP
By default, both the hexadecimal key sequence and the key mapping are
shown.

.SH EXAMPLES
Wait for user input while monitoring media playback:

.PP
.nf
url=$(selectmusicstream)
duration=$(send_to_upnp_speaker "${url}")

while true
do
    key=$(shakey -t "${duration}" -s \\
        -p "+/Vol up -/Vol down")

    if [ "${key}" = "q" ]; then
        break
    fi

    if [ "${key}" = "+" ]; then
        incvolume
    fi

    if [ "${key}" = "-" ]; then
        decvolume
    fi

    duration=$(get_remaining_duration)
done
.fi

.PP
Request confirmation from the user:

.PP
.nf
file="file.txt"

answer=$(shakey -csp \\
    "Do you really want to delete ${file}? (y/n): ")
.fi

.PP
Select an entry using a single key:

.PP
.nf
printf "%s\\n%s\\n%s\\n" \\
    "1. Blue" \\
    "2. Green" \\
    "3. Red"

shakey -bsp "Select a color (1-3): "
.fi

